# Exercise 1.4: Exploring open data

From *Programming in High Energy Particle Physics*, Chapter 1

[![Open In Colab](https://colab.research.google.com/assets/colab-badge.svg)](https://colab.research.google.com/github/isildakbora/hep-programming-guide-notebooks/blob/main/ch01/ex01_4_solution.ipynb)
CELL 1 markdown
Exercise 1.4 Exploring open data Explore the CERN Open Data Portal ( https://opendata.cern.ch/ ) or the CMS Open Data records. Download a small sample dataset and inspect its structure using ROOT (or uproot in Python). Identify the main TTree branches and their types. What is the approximate size of a single event in this dataset?

<details><summary>Hint</summary>

In ROOT, open the file with TFile::Open and call tree-&gt;Print() ; in Python, uproot.open(path)["Events"].show() lists branches and types (use the tree name found in the file). Divide the file size by the number of entries to estimate the size per event.

</details>
CELL 2 code
import sys
IN_COLAB = "google.colab" in sys.modules
if IN_COLAB:
    get_ipython().run_line_magic("pip", "install -q uproot numpy")
CELL 3 markdown
### Inspect a local stand-in

This is a **toy ROOT file**, not CERN data. The [CERN Open Data Portal](https://opendata.cern.ch/) offers real records; use a verified small HTTPS file for the full open-data task.
CELL 4 code
import numpy as np, uproot
from pathlib import Path
rng = np.random.default_rng(42)
n_events = 500
with uproot.recreate("toy_events.root") as f:
    tree = f.mktree("Events", {"run": "int32", "event": "int64",
                               "Muon_pt": "float32", "Muon_eta": "float32"})
    tree.extend({"run": np.full(n_events, 1, dtype=np.int32),
                 "event": np.arange(n_events, dtype=np.int64),
                 "Muon_pt": rng.exponential(35, n_events).astype(np.float32),
                 "Muon_eta": rng.normal(0, 1, n_events).astype(np.float32)})
path = Path("toy_events.root")
CELL 5 code
with uproot.open(path) as f:
    print("Keys:", f.keys())
    tree = f["Events"]
    tree.show()
    branch_types = {name: tree[name].typename for name in tree.keys()}
    entries = tree.num_entries
bytes_per_event = path.stat().st_size / entries
print("Branches and types:", branch_types)
print(f"{entries} events, {path.stat().st_size} bytes, {bytes_per_event:.1f} bytes/event")
assert entries == n_events and bytes_per_event > 0
CELL 6 markdown
### Interpretation

The file contains `run`, `event`, `Muon_pt`, and `Muon_eta`. File bytes divided by entries includes metadata and compression, so it is only an approximate stored size per event. **Partial support:** the open-data download is left as a documented extension; the notebook runs offline with synthetic data.

